{
  "id": "ep090",
  "duration": 55.807,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 4.456,
      "text": "A crashed space probe fell silent, yet spoke from Venus weeks later.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "1972 CPA 4166.jpg",
        "credit": "USSR Post",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1972_CPA_4166.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 4.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 4.456,
      "end": 11.651,
      "text": "On December 15, 1970, the Soviet Union's Venera 7 reached the dark night side of Venus.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "097 101 surface date 2016 07 12 04 05.jpg",
        "credit": "Meli thev",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:097_101_surface_date_2016_07_12_04_05.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 4.467,
          "end": 11.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 11.651,
      "end": 19.096,
      "text": "Engineers built the lander with a titanium pressure shell and chilled its interior to survive the extreme environment.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Cut-away model of a Soviet Venera 12 lander.jpg",
        "credit": "Don S. Montgomery, U.S. Navy (Ret.)",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cut-away_model_of_a_Soviet_Venera_12_lander.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 11.667,
          "end": 14.6,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 14.6,
          "end": 19.1,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 19.096,
      "end": 26.066,
      "text": "But during descent, the parachute failed, causing the probe to strike the surface at seventeen meters per second.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artistic impression of the Venusian surface and atmosphere.jpg",
        "credit": "ESO/M. Kornmesser/L. Cal\u00e7ada",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artistic_impression_of_the_Venusian_surface_and_atmosphere.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 19.1,
          "end": 23.967,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 23.967,
          "end": 26.067,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 26.066,
      "end": 33.566,
      "text": "Mission controllers initially believed the spacecraft died instantly when it fell completely silent on impact.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Venera 9 Space Probe 01.jpg",
        "credit": "Stolbovsky",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venera_9_Space_Probe_01.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 26.067,
          "end": 29.3,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 29.3,
          "end": 33.567,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 33.566,
      "end": 41.511,
      "text": "Weeks later, scientists reviewing recorded radio tapes discovered a faint, twenty-three minute signal hidden beneath the static.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "The Soviet Union 1988 CPA 6018 stamp (14th standard issue of Soviet Union. 1st issue. Setting up long-range space communications. Radio telescope) 1200dpi.jpg",
        "credit": "USSR Post (\u0440\u0438\u0441\u0443\u043d\u043e\u043a \u0412. \u042d. \u041a\u043e\u0432\u0430\u043b\u044f, \u0433\u0440\u0430\u0432\u044e\u0440\u0430 \u043d\u0430 \u043c\u0435\u0442\u0430\u043b\u043b\u0435 \u0413. \u0424. \u0417\u043e\u0442\u043e\u0432\u0430).",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Soviet_Union_1988_CPA_6018_stamp_(14th_standard_issue_of_Soviet_Union._1st_issue._Setting_up_long-range_space_communications._Radio_telescope)_1200dpi.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 33.567,
          "end": 41.5,
          "kind": "cover",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 41.511,
      "end": 50.256,
      "text": "The transmissions confirmed a crushing pressure ninety times that of Earth and a temperature of four hundred seventy-five degrees Celsius.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Space probe Venera on the venus surface (artist recreation).jpg",
        "credit": "wewewegrafikbaydeh (Reimund Bertrams)",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Space_probe_Venera_on_the_venus_surface_(artist_recreation).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 41.5,
          "end": 44.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 44.667,
          "end": 50.267,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 50.256,
      "end": 55.457,
      "text": "Those faint tapes proved that the silent spacecraft had actually survived to make history.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "1972 CPA 4166.jpg",
        "credit": "USSR Post",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1972_CPA_4166.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 50.267,
          "end": 55.8,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
